# Resolution
Fixed in commit `e443424 Clean up direct put pending visibility errors`.

The direct PUT `MetadataCommandLogConflict` branch now handles
`pending_metadata_command_for_bucket` errors explicitly instead of using `?`.
If the pending-visibility read fails before metadata-command ownership has been
transferred, `commit_direct_put_object_from_payload_shards` first runs
`cleanup_direct_put_attempt_before_command_ownership!()` and only then returns
the mapped error. This preserves the existing pre-command cleanup invariant for
the caller-owned bucket write proof and unowned direct PUT payload shards.

The fix added regression coverage in
`direct_put_log_conflict_pending_visibility_error_cleans_new_payload`, which
forces a command-id conflict followed by a malformed pending-slot visibility
error after direct PUT shards have been written. The test verifies that the
bucket write reservation is released and the newly written payload shard files
are deleted.

# Summary
Introduced bug: the new `pending_visible = self.pending_metadata_command_for_bucket(...)?` statement in the direct PUT log-conflict branch can early-return without invoking the existing cleanup path for already-written shards and reservations.
In `commit_direct_put_object_from_payload_shards`, direct PUT payload shards have already been written before metadata commit handling begins. The function defines `cleanup_direct_put_attempt_before_command_ownership!()` to release the bucket write proof, release the object generation reservation, and delete the just-written payload shards on failures before a metadata command owns them. This commit changed the `MetadataCommandLogConflict` handling path to first call `pending_metadata_command_for_bucket(...)?` to decide whether a pending command is visible. Because this uses `?` directly, any error from that metadata read returns from the function immediately and bypasses the cleanup macro. The old code routed errors from `drain_one_pending_object_metadata_command` through an `if let Err(error)` block that performed cleanup before returning. As a result, under a log-conflict plus pending-metadata-read failure, the server can leave orphaned payload shards and leaked bucket/object generation reservations. Repeated failures can consume disk space and leave stale write state, impacting availability.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm direct PUT payload shards are written before metadata commit ownership is established.
- [x] Confirm the cleanup macro is the intended pre-command-ownership cleanup path for direct PUT failures.
- [x] Confirm the log-conflict branch contains a direct `?` on `pending_metadata_command_for_bucket` before cleanup is invoked.
- [x] Dynamically trigger a log conflict followed by a pending-command visibility/read error after shards and reservations exist.
- [x] Verify observable leaked state after the error: shard files remain and bucket write reservation remains; object generation reservation was also observed remaining.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC. Code review confirmed the vulnerable control flow: direct PUT shards are written before metadata commit (`crates/server-core/src/coordinator/put.rs:155-163`, commit invoked at `:245-249`). The intended cleanup macro releases the caller bucket write proof, best-effort releases object generation reservation, and deletes direct PUT shard files (`crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:4028-4045`). In the new log-conflict branch, `pending_metadata_command_for_bucket(pg_id, &req.bucket)?` is executed before the cleanup-guarded `drain_after_object_pg_log_conflict` path (`crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:4133-4148`), so an error there returns immediately. I added a PoC test using the existing `before_direct_put_command_id` hook to race in a malformed pending slot after the initial pending-slot check but before command construction. The slot is valid enough for `next_metadata_command_id_from_locked_pg_at_least()` to report `MetadataCommandLogConflict` (`crates/storage/src/node_client.rs:6288-6297`), but `pending_metadata_command_envelope()` then fails due a mismatched scope bucket (`crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:2620-2627`). Running `cargo test -p storage poc_direct_put_log_conflict_pending_visibility_error_should_cleanup -- --nocapture` produced a reproducible panic showing cleanup state leaked: `leaked_shards=3; bucket_write_reservation_leaked=true; generation_reservation_leaked=true`. Existing nearby cleanup-oriented test `zero_apply_direct_put_commit_records_tombstone_and_cleans_new_payload` passed before the PoC, confirming the build/test environment was functional. Valgrind was attempted but not installed. lldb batch execution reproduced the same test failure and backtrace to the PoC assertion.

